# The ledger of `containers/trie`: a prefix tree of texts to values, what
# Python's `pygtrie` 2.6.2 (`Trie`, `CharTrie`, `StringTrie`, `PrefixSet`),
# Rust's `radix_trie` 0.3.0 (`Trie`, `SubTrie`, `SubTrieMut`) and Java's
# Commons Collections 4.6.0 `PatriciaTrie` offer, one block per capability,
# and what Heroes answers to each. Go 1.27, Rust 1.100 and Python 3.15 have no
# trie in their standard libraries; the lists came from `dir()` of pygtrie
# installed in the scratch folder, from `cargo doc` of radix_trie there, and
# from the Javadoc of PatriciaTrie 4.6.0 (no Java runtime is on this machine).
#
# A record takes no type parameter, so the structure is a shape holding the
# keys, which are texts, beside the program's own value list, lent with `@`
# to every write of a map; a set of texts has no list at all.

## The trie

capability trie/make  an empty trie of texts to values, or of texts alone
  heroes  trie.new_map, trie.new_set
  go      -
  rust    Trie::new, Trie::default
  python  Trie, CharTrie, StringTrie, PrefixSet, Trie.fromkeys
  java    PatriciaTrie.PatriciaTrie
  note    a radix tree, a branch only where two keys part, each label read from a key held under it, so no label is copied and a key's bytes are kept once; radix_trie and PatriciaTrie are radix trees too, pygtrie a node a character, unsorted unless `enable_sorting`. A map's values are the program's list, a set has none, as `containers/ordered` keeps them. `fromkeys` is a loop of `trie.put`

capability trie/size  how many keys a trie holds
  heroes  trie.size
  go      -
  rust    Trie::len, Trie::is_empty
  python  Trie.__len__, Trie.__bool__, PrefixSet.__len__
  java    size, isEmpty
  note    -

capability trie/put  a value put under a text, replacing the one held
  heroes  trie.put
  go      -
  rust    Trie::insert, Trie::map_with_default, SubTrieMut::insert
  python  Trie.__setitem__, Trie.setdefault, Trie.update, Trie.merge
  java    put, putAll, putIfAbsent, compute, computeIfAbsent, computeIfPresent, merge, replace, replaceAll
  note    replaces the value of a key held, as all three do; Rust's `insert` answers the value it replaced (observed `Some(1)`), which a program reads at `trie.slot_of` first. `setdefault`, `map_with_default`, `putIfAbsent` and the `compute` family are `trie.slot_of` then a write at the slot or `trie.put`; `update`, `merge` and `putAll` are a loop of `trie.put`

capability trie/add  a text added to a trie of texts alone, and taken out
  heroes  trie.add, trie.discard
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  PrefixSet.add, PrefixSet.discard, PrefixSet.remove, PrefixSet.pop
  java    -
  note    answers whether the key was new or was there, as a set does. pygtrie's PrefixSet keeps only the shortest of keys that begin one another (observed: `foobar` added after `foo` changes nothing, `fo` added takes `foo` out) and its `in` asks whether a key held begins the text; here a set keeps every key it is given, and that `in` is `trie.shortest_prefix_slot`. `pop` is `trie.last_slot` then `trie.discard_at`

capability trie/get  the value under a text
  heroes  trie.get, trie.slot_of
  go      -
  rust    Trie::get, Trie::get_mut, SubTrie::get, SubTrieMut::get, Trie::value, Trie::value_mut
  python  Trie.__getitem__, Trie.get
  java    get, getOrDefault
  note    absent is `not_found`, where pygtrie raises `KeyError`, or `ShortKeyError` for a text that only begins a key (observed); a value changed in place is written at the slot `trie.slot_of` answers, the value list being the program's

capability trie/contains  whether a trie holds a text, and where
  heroes  trie.contains
  go      -
  rust    Trie::get
  python  Trie.__contains__, Trie.has_key, Trie.has_node, Trie.HAS_VALUE
  java    containsKey
  note    a text that only begins a key is not held (observed in pygtrie: `inn` held, `in` not)

capability trie/key-at  the text at a slot
  heroes  trie.key_at
  go      -
  rust    Trie::key, SubTrie::key
  python  -
  java    -
  note    the trie keeps its keys, so a slot names its key at no cost

capability trie/remove  a text and its value taken out
  heroes  trie.remove, trie.remove_at, trie.discard_at
  go      -
  rust    Trie::remove, SubTrieMut::remove
  python  Trie.pop, Trie.__delitem__, Trie.popitem
  java    remove
  note    answers the value taken out, as Rust's `remove` (observed `Some(5)`) and Python's `pop` (observed 1); absent is `not_found`. The last slot moves into the one freed, as in every structure of `containers`. `popitem` is `trie.last_slot` then `trie.remove_at`

capability trie/has-prefix  whether some key begins with a text
  heroes  trie.has_prefix
  go      -
  rust    Trie::get_raw_descendant, Trie::subtrie, SubTrie::is_empty
  python  Trie.has_subtrie, Trie.has_node, Trie.HAS_SUBTRIE
  java    prefixMap
  note    true for a key equal to the prefix as well, as radix_trie's `get_raw_descendant` (observed: a subtrie of 1 for `tea`) and Java's `prefixMap`; pygtrie's `has_subtrie` is false where nothing stands under the key (observed: false for `tea`, true for `te`), and its `has_node` answers both questions in one number, which `trie.contains` and this answer apart. The empty prefix asks whether any key is held (observed: false on an empty pygtrie)

capability trie/with-prefix  every key that begins with a text, in order
  heroes  trie.keys_with_prefix, trie.slots_with_prefix
  go      -
  rust    Trie::subtrie, Trie::subtrie_mut, Trie::iter, Trie::keys, Trie::values, SubTrie::iter, SubTrie::keys, SubTrie::values, SubTrieMut::iter, SubTrieMut::keys, SubTrieMut::values
  python  Trie.keys, Trie.iterkeys, Trie.items, Trie.iteritems, Trie.values, Trie.itervalues, Trie.__iter__, Trie.__getitem__ with a slice, Trie.enable_sorting, PrefixSet.iter, PrefixSet.__iter__
  java    prefixMap, keySet, values, entrySet, mapIterator
  note    in byte order, the order of radix_trie, of PatriciaTrie and of pygtrie with `enable_sorting` (observed: te, tea, ted, ten); a prefix no key begins with gives an empty list, where pygtrie raises `KeyError` (observed). Values and items are read at the slots

capability trie/count-prefix  how many keys begin with a text
  heroes  trie.count_with_prefix
  go      -
  rust    SubTrie::len
  python  -
  java    prefixMap
  note    kept at every node, so it costs the prefix's length; radix_trie's `subtrie("te").len()` answered 4 for te, tea, ted and ten, and so does this

capability trie/remove-prefix  every key that begins with a text taken out
  heroes  trie.remove_with_prefix, trie.discard_with_prefix
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  Trie.__delitem__ with a slice
  java    prefixMap
  note    answers how many keys went; observed pygtrie's `del t["te":]` over to, tea, ted and ten leaving to, as here

capability trie/longest-prefix  the longest key that begins a text
  heroes  trie.longest_prefix_slot
  go      -
  rust    Trie::get_ancestor, Trie::get_ancestor_value, Trie::get_raw_ancestor
  python  Trie.longest_prefix
  java    -
  note    it takes the byte `from` where the key would begin, so a tokenizer or a router scanning a text never cuts a slice of it; pygtrie's `longest_prefix` and radix_trie's `get_ancestor` both answered `ant` for `anteats` over a, an, ant, eat and s (observed), as here; the empty key, held, begins every text (observed in pygtrie)

capability trie/shortest-prefix  the shortest key that begins a text
  heroes  trie.shortest_prefix_slot
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  Trie.shortest_prefix, PrefixSet.__contains__
  java    -
  note    observed pygtrie answering `a` for `anteats`, as here

capability trie/prefixes  every key that begins a text, the shortest first
  heroes  trie.prefix_slots
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  Trie.prefixes, Trie.walk_towards
  java    -
  note    the shortest first, as pygtrie's `prefixes` (observed: a, an, ant; and /, /api, /api/v1 for /api/v1/users)

capability trie/separator  prefixes counted in parts cut by a separator, as a router reads a path
  heroes  trie.prefix_slots
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  StringTrie
  java    -
  note    a StringTrie counts prefixes in parts cut by its separator (observed: of `/a` and `/ab`, only `/ab` begins `/ab/c`); here the keys stay text and `trie.prefix_slots` gives every key that begins the text, of which the program keeps the ones whose next byte in the text is the separator or the end, as its doc comment says

capability trie/order  the first and the last key, and the key after or before one
  heroes  trie.first_slot, trie.last_slot, trie.next_slot, trie.previous_slot
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  java    firstKey, lastKey, nextKey, previousKey, firstEntry, lastEntry
  note    in byte order, walked through the parent links with no stack

capability trie/range  the keys between two texts in byte order
  heroes  refused: the keys between two texts in byte order are an ordered set's question, which `containers/ordered` answers with `ordered.slots_between`, `ordered.ceiling_slot` and `ordered.floor_slot` in the logarithm of the size; a trie's own range is a prefix, `trie.slots_with_prefix`. Wrong the day a program needs both prefixes and byte ranges over keys it cannot keep twice
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  java    headMap, tailMap, subMap, comparator
  note    -

capability trie/nearest-by-bits  the key nearest a text by the exclusive or of their bits
  heroes  refused: the key nearest a text by the exclusive or of their bits is PatriciaTrie's own measure of its bit-wise tree and orders texts by no rule a reader can state; the keys nearest in byte order are `trie.previous_slot` and `trie.next_slot` around a text added to a set for the asking. Wrong where a program's keys are numbers whose bits are a metric, which a trie of texts does not hold
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  java    select, selectKey, selectValue
  note    -

capability trie/byte-keys  keys of bytes that are not text
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    TrieKey, TrieKey::encode, TrieKey::encode_bytes, NibbleVec
  python  Trie over any sequence
  java    getKeyAnalyzer, KeyAnalyzer
  note    the walks compare bytes already; a key of bytes that are not text needs the keys kept as `[u8]`, which a key read back as text would then have to validate, and a second face of every function that takes a key. Not written badly in the time of this round: texts cover routers, completion and tokenizers over text, and `bytes` finds byte patterns anywhere

capability trie/nodes  the tree's own nodes walked: children, leaves, the prefix a node stands for
  heroes  refused: the nodes are the package's own and change shape at every write, so a program that walked them would hold what the next call breaks; what a walk of them computes, the keys under a prefix and their count, is `trie.keys_with_prefix` and `trie.count_with_prefix`, and pygtrie's `traverse` folds a function the caller builds per node, which needs a closure the language does not have. Wrong where a program needs the shape of the tree itself, a compressed dictionary written to a file
  go      -
  rust    Trie::children, Trie::is_leaf, Trie::prefix, SubTrie::children, SubTrie::is_leaf, SubTrie::prefix, SubTrieMut::children, SubTrieMut::is_leaf, SubTrieMut::prefix
  python  Trie.traverse
  java    -
  note    -

capability trie/equal  two tries compared by their keys and values
  heroes  trie.keys_with_prefix
  go      -
  rust    Trie::eq, Trie::ne
  python  Trie.__eq__, Trie.strictly_equals
  java    equals
  note    two tries hold the same keys where `trie.keys_in_order` agree, and the same values where the values at their slots do; `==` compares the shapes node by node, which two built in other orders may not share, as Python's `strictly_equals` against its `==`

capability trie/copy  a trie copied, and emptied
  heroes  trie.clear, trie.clear_set
  go      -
  rust    Trie::clone, Trie::clone_from
  python  Trie.copy, Trie.clear, PrefixSet.copy, PrefixSet.clear
  java    clear
  note    a value is a copy (spec § 3), so `b = a` copies a trie with nothing more; a map's and a set's emptying apart, as `put` and `add` are

## Dropped

dropped trie-plumbing  iterator types, formatting, conversions and the traits every Rust type carries, a Python mapping's abstract base, a Java map's views and hashing: the trie's own answers are lists and slots
  go      -
  rust    radix_trie::iter::Iter, radix_trie::iter::Keys, radix_trie::iter::Values, radix_trie::iter::Children, SubTrieResult, Trie::from_iter, Trie::fmt, and on Trie `borrow`, `borrow_mut`, `into`, `from`, `try_from`, `try_into`, `type_id`, `to_owned`, `clone_into`, `clone_to_uninit`
  python  Trie.__repr__, Trie.__str__, ShortKeyError, NodeFactory, PrefixSet.isdisjoint
  java    hashCode, toString, clone, containsValue, forEach
